<edge source="global_financial_stress::project_financing_cost" target="euro_security_autonomy::industrial_investment" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.9</data>
  <data key="d36">A rise in effective project financing costs immediately increases hurdle rates for capital projects and causes corporate and public sponsors to delay or cancel planned industrial capex, reducing the flow of new industrial investment.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_financial_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">euro_security_autonomy</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_financial_stress::project_financing_cost" target="iran_regional_escalation::producer_output_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher effective costs of long‑term financing raise breakevens for development and maintenance projects, causing producers to postpone or cancel drilling and capex, which lowers production growth and aggregate output rates over the medium term.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_financial_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_financial_stress::project_financing_cost" target="russia_ukraine_war::military_support_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising project financing costs increase the price and delay of long‑lead procurement and aid‑funded industrial projects (e.g., ammunition plants, vehicle production), slowing the rate at which external materiel and capability are delivered to the conflict zone.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_financial_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_financial_stress::project_financing_cost" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::congressional_scrutiny" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising financing costs push semiconductor and AI firms to seek unconventional funding (foreign partners, mergers, state aid), which triggers congressional inquiries and oversight into capital sources and technology transfer risks.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_financial_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
